Situation:
Brigade sustainment operations are ongoing in a contested sector. The 3rd Infantry Battalion at Forward Operating Base Falcon and the 2nd Armored Squadron at Combat Outpost Ridge require ammunition, fuel, and rations to maintain combat power and freedom of maneuver. Draw is authorized from multiple rear-area depots (Supply Depot Alpha, Bravo, Charlie, and Delta). The ground network includes a main supply route with intermittent interdiction, a mountain pass subject to weather closures, and a valley corridor exposed to observation and indirect fire. Enemy activity (IEDs, ambushes), limited engineer clearance teams, and constrained escort availability drive route security demands. Air resupply is restricted to emergency use. Time and convoy discipline are critical.

Mission:
Deliver required classes of supply to the 3rd Infantry Battalion and the 2nd Armored Squadron during the current operational period while minimizing total operational cost: route transport costs, shortage penalty costs, per-vehicle deployment costs, and fixed route security risk costs associated with activating any ground line of communication.

Execution/Concept:
- Supply sources and limits: Depots may push multiple supply types, but drawdown is capped by on-hand stocks by type and period. Allocation must observe each depot’s available quantities.
- Demand and shortfall: Each unit’s demand by supply type must be met by shipments from any combination of depots. Shortfalls are permitted only when operationally unavoidable and will incur penalty costs to reflect reduced readiness.
- Vehicle capacity and loading: Convoy capacity limits the volume of mixed loads per route. Supply volume factors govern how much can be loaded per vehicle. Consolidation by type and origin is encouraged to maximize utilization.
- Route activation and security overhead: Vehicles may only move on routes that are explicitly activated for the period. Activating any depot-to-unit route triggers a fixed Risk Cost, regardless of whether one vehicle or many use it. This reflects the security overhead for escort teams, engineer route clearance, ISR coverage, and QRF posture.
- Fleet limits and costs: Total vehicles employed across all routes cannot exceed the available fleet. Each deployed vehicle incurs a per-vehicle cost in addition to route transport costs.
- Cost-informed routing: Transport costs vary by depot-to-unit pair based on distance, terrain, and threat. High-threat corridors may have lower pure transport costs but higher risk overheads. The plan will consolidate shipments on the fewest feasible routes to amortize fixed risk costs, leveraging safer or cheaper corridors when depot stocks and capacities allow.
- Priorities and contingencies: Ammunition and fuel to the engaged battalion take priority. Rations and non-critical items may be deferred if penalties are lower than the combined transport and risk costs. An alternate route will be identified but only activated if required to mitigate disruption.
- Coordination: Movement Control teams will sequence convoys; Engineers/EOD will clear and certify activated routes; Military Police will provide escorts commensurate with the threat. Intelligence and aviation assets will support route security during activation windows.

